{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\n`CodeHealthConfig.enabled` (`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:538`, default `False`) has ZERO read\nsites in `src/rebar`. Nothing gates on it.\n\nA code trace of the live path confirms it: `_commands/metrics.py:139-148` loads\n`config.load_config(root).code_health` and carries only `scan_roots` (:144), `size_cap` (:145) and\n`size_near_fraction` (:146) into the metrics context. `enabled` is never consulted, so the\nstructural metrics (`module_size_distribution`, `oversized_module_count`, `complexity_summary`,\n`duplication_summary`) evaluate even at the default `enabled=False`.\n\nThe field is documented and settable, so an operator can set `enabled = false` and reasonably\nbelieve code-health analysis is off. It is not.\n\n## Why it was never caught\n\nThe 6754 inert-config gate cannot see this one -- see the sibling bug on that gate. It matches\nbare attribute NAMES globally, and `.enabled` is read on other configs (`ui.enabled`,\n`LangfuseConfig`, under `llm/`), so this field passes without ever being read itself. That is also\nwhy it carries no `# read-via:` marker while the neighbouring `analyzers` field needed one.\n\n## The decision this needs\n\nSame shape as dce2: either HONOR the flag (gate structural metric evaluation on it) or DELETE it.\nHonoring it is a behavioral change -- metrics that run today would stop running for anyone on the\ndefault -- so this needs an explicit operator ruling, not an agent's judgment.\n\nNote the interaction with dce2's ruling: the operator chose to DELETE the inert `analyzers` field\nrather than wire it, with intent preserved as idea 94bc-a0b4-bc37-47b2. A consistent ruling here\nis plausible but must not be assumed, because the two differ -- deleting `analyzers` removes a knob\nthat never did anything, whereas deleting `enabled` removes the only advertised off switch for the\nwhole code-health subsystem.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An explicit operator decision is recorded: honor `enabled` or remove it.\n- [ ] If HONORED: structural metric evaluation is gated on it, with a test proving the metrics do\n      NOT evaluate at `enabled=False` and DO at `enabled=True`; the default's behavior change is\n      called out in the changelog.\n- [ ] If REMOVED: the field, its coercion row and its config-surface entries go, and the docs no\n      longer advertise an off switch that does not exist.\n- [ ] Either way, a guard prevents it silently regressing to inert again (this depends on the 6754\n      gate fix -- a same-named field cannot be policed until that lands).\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound by a code-trace archaeology pass over the metrics/code-health system while scoping dce2.\n\n## Operator ruling — RECORDED\n\n**Decision: REMOVE `CodeHealthConfig.enabled`.** Ruled by the operator on 2026-08-05 and\nrecorded as the first comment on this ticket (\"OPERATOR DECISION 2026-08-05: REMOVE the\nfield\", author `RebarBotNava`, `author_id` `594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d`) — that comment is the\ncitable attestation this AC closes against.\n\nThe counter-argument was surfaced to the operator before the ruling: this differs from dce2\nbecause deleting `analyzers` removed a knob that never did anything, whereas deleting\n`enabled` removes the only advertised OFF SWITCH for the whole code-health subsystem. The\noperator considered that tradeoff and chose removal knowingly. This is a one-way door for the\nadvertised switch; the intent it expressed is not being preserved as a follow-up idea (unlike\ndce2, whose analyzer-dispatch intent survives as idea 94bc-a0b4-bc37-47b2) because there is no\nsurviving design for a code-health off switch — re-adding one later would be a fresh feature\ndecision, not a restoration. Do not re-litigate the ruling.\n\n## Fix plan (REMOVAL, per the recorded operator ruling above)\n\nPure removal — the field controls nothing today (zero read sites), so deleting it cannot\nchange what runs.\n\n1. `src/rebar/_config_schema.py` — delete the `enabled: bool = False` field from\n   `CodeHealthConfig`; delete its `\"enabled\": lambda v, k: _as_bool(v, k)` coercion row from\n   the `code_health` entry of `_SECTIONS`; refresh the class docstring, which no longer\n   describes an inert-by-default toggle.\n2. `src/rebar/_deprecations.py` — add TWO warn-class tombstones to `_TOMBSTONE_REGISTRY` so a\n   still-set input WARNs and exits 0 rather than being silently dropped: an `env` tombstone for\n   `REBAR_CODE_HEALTH_ENABLED` and a `cfg` tombstone for `code_health.enabled`. This is\n   mandatory, not optional: `tests/unit/test_config_surface_golden.py` fails the build when a\n   golden-pinned key exists in neither the live schema nor the tombstone registry.\n3. `docs/config.md` — drop `enabled` from both TOML examples and from the prose type list in\n   the `code_health.*` section (so the docs stop advertising an off switch that does not\n   exist), and add both tombstoned inputs to the warn-class list.\n4. `tests/unit/test_code_health_config.py` — drop only the plain `value.enabled is False` /\n   `value.enabled is True` assertions from the two happy-path tests.\n5. `tests/unit/test_code_health_config_heldout.py` — drop the `-c code_health.enabled=true`\n   CLI override argument, the `enabled` entries in the composed config/sources assertions, and\n   `code_health.enabled` / `REBAR_CODE_HEALTH_ENABLED` from the golden-surface expectation\n   sets.\n6. `tests/scripts/test_metrics_docs.py` — drop `enabled` from the documented-keys tuple.\n7. `tests/unit/test_config_backcompat.py` — add a RUNTIME test (the home of the existing\n   tombstone/warn tests) asserting the new tombstones actually fire: loading a config with\n   `[code_health] enabled = true` set, and separately with `REBAR_CODE_HEALTH_ENABLED` set,\n   emits the warn-class removed-input message and does NOT raise (warn class, not error\n   class). Registry presence alone is not proof — the warn path is verified by execution.\n\nNot touched: `tests/golden/config_surface.json` stays pinned (that is the point of the golden\n— the tombstones, not a golden edit, account for the removed entries).\n\n`_as_bool` is NOT orphaned by this removal (dozens of other coercion rows in `_SECTIONS` use\nit), unlike dce2's `_as_str_dict`.\n\n### How the removal is proved\n\n- `grep -rn \"code_health.enabled\\|REBAR_CODE_HEALTH_ENABLED\" src/rebar` returns nothing.\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_config_surface_golden.py tests/unit/test_config_backcompat.py\n  tests/unit/test_code_health_config.py tests/unit/test_code_health_config_heldout.py` passes.\n- `rebar config validate` with the key still set exits 0 and reports it as a removed input.\n\n### Evidence of no behavior change\n\n`rebar metrics` before and after produces the same structural metrics (`module_size_distribution`,\n`oversized_module_count`, `complexity_summary`, `duplication_summary`). A byte-identical whole-output\ndiff is NOT achievable — two consecutive runs already differ in time- and store-driven ticket metrics\n(dce2 found the same) — so the evidence is per-metric attribution over the code_health lens.\n\n### On the guard AC\n\nMOOT for a deleted field: there is nothing left to regress to inert. It also depended on bug\n2c58-e710-275e-4ff7 regardless — the current inert-config gate could never have caught this\nfield, because `.enabled` is read on other configs (`ui.enabled`, `LangfuseConfig`).\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785911995712017001,"uuid":"ef062da7-4b1b-49eb-bbdd-f9bb83817548"}